--- WRITEUP/note/symmetric_es7.tex lines 16-27
  16: \begin{abstract}
  17: Let $ES(7)$ be the least $n$ such that every $n$ points in general position in the plane contain $7$ points in convex
  18: position; the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres conjecture predicts $ES(7)=33$. We show, with computer-assisted proofs, that every
  19: $33$-point set in general position with a threefold rotational symmetry contains a convex heptagon, and that every
  20: mirror-symmetric set of at least $33$ points does. Together with explicit examples, the largest threefold-symmetric and
  21: mirror-symmetric sets without a convex heptagon have exactly $31$ and $32$ points. Combined with the fourfold result of
  22: Subercaseaux, Mackey, Qian and Heule and a fivefold exclusion, the only symmetric sets of at least $33$ points that could
  23: still avoid a convex heptagon are centrally symmetric sets of even size at least $34$. As companion material we record
  24: exact maxima for other symmetry types, with their antecedents, and an explicit centrally symmetric $32$-point example,
  25: which is a special case of a construction of Valtr described by Baek and Balko. None of this settles $ES(7)$ or improves
  26: its upper bound.
  27: \end{abstract}
