--- WRITEUP/note/symmetric_es7.tex lines 242-251
 242: \section{What remains open}
 243: $ES(7)$ itself is open, and no unrestricted bound is improved here. By Corollary~\ref{cor:only}, the remaining symmetric
 244: route to a counterexample is a centrally symmetric set of even size at least $34$; excluding $34$ points would exclude
 245: all of them. For the analogous encoding with the centre $c$ as an element ($v_c=(v_p+v_{-p})/2$, so
 246: $\chi(c,p,q)=\chi(-p,p,q)$), a split by the convex hull into $136$ cases has a certified cover. All $120$
 247: hexagonal-hull cases are refuted, each with a retained proof accepted by both checkers, so a centrally symmetric $34$-point counterexample would
 248: have a parallelogram hull. The parallelogram cases are open; nested parallelograms, the layer structure of the extremal $32$-point sets,
 249: are the hard core (\texttt{C2C34}). An abstract centre imposes no metric midpoint condition and no
 250: realizability: the fourfold $32$-point formula of the same kind is satisfiable, as~\cite{SMQH25} also observed for its own
 251: model, although no real set exists. Only an unsatisfiable answer, with a justified case cover, would settle the case.
